From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:01:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t8132-j604: expose the boot NOR nvram,
 read-only

Enable the T8132 QSPIMC on the J604 with its SPI NOR and one
fixed partition, nvram, at the offset and size of the J604 ADT's
/arm-io/qspi/spinor/anvram (0x700000 +0x100000, MEASURED runtime ADT).
The partition is read-only in the device tree, and spi-apple-qspi keeps
T8132 controllers read-only with no write candidate for this board, so
nothing can program or erase the NOR. The firmware (0x20000..) and
syscfg (0x680000..) regions are not described at all.

spi-max-frequency 10 MHz is a conservative rate (Aurora's T8132 board
dtsi uses the same; INFERENCE that the J604 part accepts it, as every
SPI NOR does for 0x03 reads at 10 MHz). No vcc-supply: the ADT names
none and the core falls back to a dummy regulator.

This gives Linux read access to the NVRAM the startup-disk tools read
(asahi-nvram/aurora-bless style). NVRAM writes stay off until the J604
JEDEC id and a read-back match are measured on this machine.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
index bee83f0b2a..4e6135cece 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t8132-j604.dts
@@ -1027,3 +1027,35 @@
 
 /* The HDMI port, on dcpext0. */
 #include "t8132-j604-hdmi.dtsi"
+
+/*
+ * Boot NOR, read-only. ADT /arm-io/qspi/spinor ("nor-flash,spi"), child
+ * anvram ("nvram,nor") at 0x700000 +0x100000 (MEASURED, J604 runtime ADT).
+ * Only the nvram partition is described, and it is marked read-only: the
+ * firmware and syscfg regions are never exposed, and spi-apple-qspi keeps
+ * T8132 read-only (no J604 write admission). The NVRAM write path is a
+ * separate change after a JEDEC read and a read-back test on this machine.
+ */
+&qspi {
+	status = "okay";
+
+	flash@0 {
+		compatible = "jedec,spi-nor";
+		reg = <0>;
+		spi-max-frequency = <10000000>;
+		spi-rx-bus-width = <1>;
+		spi-tx-bus-width = <1>;
+
+		partitions {
+			compatible = "fixed-partitions";
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <1>;
+
+			nvram: partition@700000 {
+				label = "nvram";
+				reg = <0x700000 0x100000>;
+				read-only;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
